<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard M. Eaton</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Richard Eaton</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard M. Eaton is an American historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, long a professor at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>. His books include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> (1978), The Rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Frontier, 1204–1760 (1993), which linked the spread of Islam in eastern Bengal to agrarian settlement on the forest frontier, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the Persianate Age (2019).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-eaton.jpg" alt="Richard M. Eaton" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ফয়জুল লতিফ চৌধুরী · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%A7%A8%E0%A7%A6%E0%A7%A7%E0%A7%AC_%E0%A6%B8%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B2%E0%A7%87_%E0%A6%A2%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%95%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BC_%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%9A%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B0%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%A1_%E0%A6%87%E0%A6%9F%E0%A6%A8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
